1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,917
The Map of Observing the Mysteries of the Heaven and Earth (Liangyi xuanlan tu 兩儀玄覽圖 ),

2
00:00:06,917 --> 00:00:13,375
which we call here the Xuantu is a worldmap in eight panels created in

3
00:00:13,375 --> 00:00:18,250
1603 by the Ming dynasty military official Li Yingshi李應試.

4
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:24,042
Figure 1: Map of Observing the Mysteries of the Heaven and the Earth.

5
00:00:24,042 --> 00:00:29,917
Liaoning Provincial Museum辽宁省博物馆, Shenyang, People's Republic of China

6
00:00:29,917 --> 00:00:37,000
There is strong reason to believe that the Manchus obtained this particular map before

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,917
they conquered China, a surprising development that makes us reconsider the Manchus'

8
00:00:43,917 --> 00:00:50,917
relationship to Western cartography. This interactive websiste will tell the story of

9
00:00:50,917 --> 00:00:58,708
this maps and presents some of the evidence linking the Xuantu map to the pre- conquest Manchus

10
00:00:58,708 --> 00:01:05,250
Matteo Ricci and the scholar Li Zhizao 李之藻 created in 1602 a map named Complete

11
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:11,125
Map of the Myriad Kingdoms of the World ( Kunyu Wanguo Quantu 坤輿萬國全圖).

12
00:01:11,125 --> 00:01:18,208
It included geographical descriptions of the historical five continents, complete with

13
00:01:18,208 --> 00:01:25,375
place-names, a précis of Ptolemaic cosmology, and paper instruments for measuring polar

14
00:01:25,375 --> 00:01:32,250
altitude and latitude. Figure 2: Map of myriad countries of the Earth, Kunyu wanguo

15
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:37,333
quantu 坤輿萬國全圖, by Matteo Ricci, Li Zhizao 李之藻, et al., 1602.

16
00:01:37,333 --> 00:01:41,583
James Ford Bell Library, University of Minnesota.

17
00:01:41,583 --> 00:01:48,458
The Xuantu is a large object, measuring 2 meters (78.74 inches) vertically and 4.42

18
00:01:48,458 --> 00:01:55,375
meters (174.01 inches) horizontally. As such, it fits awkwardly among Chinese prints

19
00:01:55,375 --> 00:02:02,250
from the Ming dynasty. Maps of this size meant for a high-end audience of officials

20
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:09,500
were rarely prints. Instead, just as for paintings, Chinese artisans normally used silk,

21
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:13,500
which was favored for its superior durability.

22
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:20,375
Silk, however, because of its cost, was more appropriate for the creation of unique

23
00:02:20,375 --> 00:02:24,083
items, such as decorative folding screens.

24
00:02:24,083 --> 00:02:31,250
For Sino-Western world maps, the desire to quickly disseminate many copies trumped that

25
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:38,250
of creating solid, durable objects. World maps by Matteo Ricci and other Jesuits thus

26
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:41,708
came to occupy an important niche.

27
00:02:41,708 --> 00:02:48,167
They were as large in size as silk maps made for important people, yet easily

28
00:02:48,167 --> 00:02:55,083
reproducible and affordable. This was at the root of their success in the late Ming.

29
00:02:55,083 --> 00:02:59,542
It is also the reason why so few copies survived:

30
00:02:59,542 --> 00:03:06,458
large prints were fragile. Folding screen with figures in a landscape, Qing dynasty,

31
00:03:06,458 --> 00:03:09,917
Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York.

32
00:03:09,917 --> 00:03:16,375
Indeed, Xuantu is important not so much for what it has in it but for

33
00:03:16,375 --> 00:03:23,833
what it has on it: the repairs, modifications, and annotations added to the original print.

34
00:03:23,833 --> 00:03:31,000
Material alterations, the content of annotations, and the form of the Manchu script are

35
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,167
three aspects that demonstrate the epistemological engagement of the Manchu translators

36
00:03:38,167 --> 00:03:45,417
of the Later Jin state with the Map of Observing the Mysteries of the Heaven and Earth.

37
00:03:45,417 --> 00:03:52,500
The Map of Observing Mysteries, like its 1602 predecessor, the Map of Myriad Kingdoms,

38
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:59,500
was intended by Ricci to be colored by hand, to better distinguish the continents and

39
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:02,958
land masses from the bodies of water.

40
00:04:02,958 --> 00:04:09,750
Since the coloring process had to be done after the printing and involved the work

41
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:16,625
of another specialized artisan, incurring additional expenses, many copies remained

42
00:04:16,625 --> 00:04:22,250
black-and-white prints. The Shenyang copy was originally uncolored.

43
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:29,417
Yet during its life in the Shenyang palace, an artisan was tasked with embellishing the

44
00:04:29,417 --> 00:04:35,667
mountain ranges across all the continents in the style of Chinese painting.

45
00:04:35,667 --> 00:04:42,917
The color scheme of these decorations—blue, green, and gold—fits the one used throughout

46
00:04:42,917 --> 00:04:46,375
the Shenyang palace.

47
00:04:46,375 --> 00:04:53,167
Besides the repairs and added decorative elements, the Shenyang copy of the Map of

48
00:04:53,167 --> 00:04:58,250
Observing Mysteries displays a number of Manchu annotations.

49
00:04:58,250 --> 00:05:04,042
These inscriptions consisted of both topology and technical elements.

50
00:05:04,042 --> 00:05:11,208
The technical elements included the cosmological theory explained in the map's prefaces

51
00:05:11,208 --> 00:05:18,208
and surrounding texts. The theory of the map expounded in the preface by Matteo Ricci

52
00:05:18,208 --> 00:05:25,000
presented relationships between Earth's spherical shape, its position in the solar

53
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,667
system, and the cycles of night and day.

54
00:05:28,667 --> 00:05:36,000
All of these features, represented by lines on the map, had Manchu translations attached.

55
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:43,083
The central text of the map summarized the cosmology in a commentary written in Manchu

56
00:05:43,083 --> 00:05:47,542
placed on the map near the line of the equator.

57
00:05:47,542 --> 00:05:54,417
The main divisions of Earth into continents and oceans, described in the preface by

58
00:05:54,417 --> 00:05:59,125
Ricci and other texts on the map, were also translated.

59
00:05:59,125 --> 00:06:06,208
Taken together, they suggest that the Manchu translator had a grasp of and an interest

60
00:06:06,208 --> 00:06:14,125
in the “theory” of the map, having read and understood the prefaces and other accompanying texts.

61
00:06:14,125 --> 00:06:20,583
There are three things that make us believe that this map stood out at its

62
00:06:20,583 --> 00:06:27,208
time. It was repaired, intentionally colored, and had inscriptions of the Manchu

63
00:06:27,208 --> 00:06:34,000
transcript on it. When it was used in the Shenyang palace, the Xuantu was damaged.

64
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,375
Winters in Shenyang are bitterly cold, and rooms must be heated.

65
00:06:39,375 --> 00:06:46,625
For this reason, there was a temperature difference between the upper and lower parts of

66
00:06:46,625 --> 00:06:53,417
the room. This created condensation, and because the map was most likely displayed

67
00:06:53,417 --> 00:06:59,042
vertically, the dampness damaged the portions closer to the ground.

68
00:06:59,042 --> 00:07:05,500
The bottom corner of the eight roll (left side of the map) of the Shengyang

69
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:08,958
copy of the Map of observing mysteries.

70
00:07:08,958 --> 00:07:15,583
This area, representing the Southern Hemisphere, was damaged and later repaired.

71
00:07:15,583 --> 00:07:22,500
An artisan had applied paper paste and, after it dried, redrew the meridians to give

72
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:29,667
the impression of continuity. Further damage to the map reveals the discrepancy between

73
00:07:29,667 --> 00:07:33,125
the two layers.

74
00:07:33,125 --> 00:07:40,042
The amount of information included in the reconstruction on the top stratum differed

75
00:07:40,042 --> 00:07:43,500
from that of the layer below.

76
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:48,292
This was due perhaps to the artisan's limited knowledge.

77
00:07:48,292 --> 00:07:54,375
But it might also be a reflection of the interests of the map owners.

78
00:07:54,375 --> 00:08:01,625
he Southern Hemisphere on the left side of the map (roll one), for instance, disappeared

79
00:08:01,625 --> 00:08:05,792
completely, being covered up in the restoration;

80
00:08:05,792 --> 00:08:12,792
only a circle and some of the radiating meridians were copied on top for consistency.

81
00:08:12,792 --> 00:08:19,958
On the other hand, the northern hemisphere was preserved, and annotations were added to

82
00:08:19,958 --> 00:08:25,333
it because it encompassed places the Manchus were interested in.

83
00:08:25,333 --> 00:08:31,958
Most translations of place-names are concentrated in East Asia, radiating toward

84
00:08:31,958 --> 00:08:38,958
Europe.The Manchus, it seems, were primarily preoccupied with locating themselves and

85
00:08:38,958 --> 00:08:45,667
their immediate surroundings; in terms of their outward interest, they focused on

86
00:08:45,667 --> 00:08:52,917
looking westward. The Manchu homeland is marked out by the translation of three adjacent

87
00:08:52,917 --> 00:08:59,833
place-names northeastof the Great Wall: Jurchen (Nüzhen女真), Mount Changbai (Changbai

88
00:08:59,833 --> 00:09:04,917
Shan長白山), and the City of the Five Nations (Wuguo Cheng五國城).

89
00:09:04,917 --> 00:09:11,542
The translation of Mount Changbai (Changbai Shan長白山), on the other hand, is more

90
00:09:11,542 --> 00:09:18,333
revealing of the Manchus' own emerging sense of identity in the sixteenth century.

91
00:09:18,333 --> 00:09:25,417
The mountain, appearing here in translation as Sanggiyan Alin, was part of an imperial

92
00:09:25,417 --> 00:09:32,208
cult throughout the Qing, revered as the birthplace of the mythical founder of the

93
00:09:32,208 --> 00:09:39,208
dynasty, Bukūri Yongšon. The addition of an in-scription alongside the mountain is an

94
00:09:39,208 --> 00:09:46,083
indication that the place was meaningful for the Manchus as early as the Later Jin.

95
00:09:46,083 --> 00:09:53,167
Moving beyond this sphere, immediately neighboring countries such as Japan, Korea, and

96
00:09:53,167 --> 00:09:57,958
the Great Ming are labeled using names of Manchu origin:

97
00:09:57,958 --> 00:10:04,833
Ose, Sohlo, and Nikan. These names do not describe places or countries but, rather,

98
00:10:04,833 --> 00:10:11,833
ethnicities.They were well established in the common geographic knowledge embedded in

99
00:10:11,833 --> 00:10:17,708
the Manchu language, as the Manchus had direct interactions with them.

100
00:10:17,708 --> 00:10:24,958
The rest of the translations of place-names on the map are transliterations in Manchu of

101
00:10:24,958 --> 00:10:31,958
the pronunciation of Chinese characters. These include the place-names for India, the

102
00:10:31,958 --> 00:10:38,958
Philippines, Java, Madagascar, the Cape of Good Hope, the Fortunate (Canary) Islands,

103
00:10:38,958 --> 00:10:46,208
Greenland,and Novaya Zemlya. The selection of these places cannot be easily explained by

104
00:10:46,208 --> 00:10:49,667
what we readily know about the Manchus.

105
00:10:49,667 --> 00:10:56,375
However, since they were singled out among all the other locations on the map, we

106
00:10:56,375 --> 00:11:03,542
must assume that they triggered a sense of relevance and recognition for the translator

107
00:11:03,542 --> 00:11:07,000
and for the map's viewership.

108
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:13,375
The translations of places like the Fortunate Islands, the Cape of Good Hope,

109
00:11:13,375 --> 00:11:20,458
Madagascar, Java, and the Philippines resulted from an awareness of the centers of the

110
00:11:20,458 --> 00:11:27,458
European trade network around Eurasia. These were, after all, places from which ships

111
00:11:27,458 --> 00:11:31,625
dropping anchor at Macao would have been coming.

112
00:11:31,625 --> 00:11:36,500
However, the form of the inscriptions is also meaningful.

113
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:43,375
They can help answer questions about the dating and authorship of the translations.

114
00:11:43,375 --> 00:11:50,250
According to the Manchu Veritable Records, the Manchu written language was a direct

115
00:11:50,250 --> 00:11:56,875
invention of bilingual scholars working for Nurhaci and was established in 1599.

116
00:11:56,875 --> 00:12:03,958
A well-known passage describes how Nurhaci noticed that Mongolian and Chinese could be

117
00:12:03,958 --> 00:12:11,042
both written and spoken, while the Manchus needed to use Mongolian—a language they did

118
00:12:11,042 --> 00:12:15,917
not speak as natives—to write documentsand proclamations.

119
00:12:15,917 --> 00:12:23,167
Consequently, Nurhaci asked the polyglot scholar Erdeni and the judge Gagai to solve the

120
00:12:23,167 --> 00:12:28,875
problem and modify the Mongolian script so as to write Manchu words.

121
00:12:28,875 --> 00:12:36,042
But because Mongolian letters could not perfectly represent Manchu sounds, this version

122
00:12:36,042 --> 00:12:39,500
of the scripthad a few drawbacks.

123
00:12:39,500 --> 00:12:45,958
The script could not clearly distinguish a and e,o and u, or k,g, and h.

124
00:12:45,958 --> 00:12:52,417
There was also no distinction between front and back d and t, and there were

125
00:12:52,417 --> 00:12:55,958
uncertainties in spelling certain words.

126
00:12:55,958 --> 00:13:03,208
A reform of the language that would address these issues was implemented during the time

127
00:13:03,208 --> 00:13:06,667
of the second Khan, Hong Taiji.

128
00:13:06,667 --> 00:13:13,375
The most important and visible aspect of this reform was the addition of dots and

129
00:13:13,375 --> 00:13:17,250
circles to solve the problem of distinction.

130
00:13:17,250 --> 00:13:23,708
The addition of dots could separate a from e,o from u, and k from g,

131
00:13:23,708 --> 00:13:27,333
while circles would distinguish k from h.

132
00:13:27,333 --> 00:13:34,125
This way of writing Manchu achieved virtually universal usage and has remained the

133
00:13:34,125 --> 00:13:37,792
standard way of writing it to this day.

134
00:13:37,792 --> 00:13:44,250
Analysis of the form of the script on the map thus indicates that the Map

135
00:13:44,250 --> 00:13:51,250
of Observing Mysterieswas translated between roughly 1625 and 1636, when the reformed

136
00:13:51,250 --> 00:13:54,958
version of the Old Manchu script was used.

137
00:13:54,958 --> 00:14:01,042
In any case, the map's annotations indicate an interest in, as well as an

138
00:14:01,042 --> 00:14:07,958
epistemological engagement with, a Sino-Western artifact in a crucial moment, as the

139
00:14:07,958 --> 00:14:12,667
Manchus were beginning to plan their conquest of China.

140
00:14:12,667 --> 00:14:19,458
